[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Inheritance in Python: A Comprehensive Guide"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inheritance is a fundamental concept in object-oriented programming (OOP) that allows you to create new classes (derived or child classes) based on existing classes (base or parent classes). The derived class inherits attributes and methods from the base class, promoting code reuse and establishing a hierarchical relationship between classes."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Inheritance Syntax"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create a derived class, you specify the base class in parentheses after the derived class name in the class definition."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"class BaseClass:
    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value

    def display_value(self):
        print(f"Value: {self.value}")

class DerivedClass(BaseClass):
    def __init__(self, value, extra_info):
        super().__init__(value)  # Call the base class's constructor
        self.extra_info = extra_info

    def display_extra_info(self):
        print(f"Extra Info: {self.extra_info}")"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, `DerivedClass` inherits from `BaseClass`. The `super().__init__(value)` call ensures that the base class's constructor is called to initialize the inherited attributes.  The derived class can then add its own attributes and methods."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"The `super()` Function"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `super()` function is used to call methods from the parent class. It's particularly useful when you want to extend or modify the behavior of a parent class method in the child class."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"class Parent:
    def method(self):
        print("Parent method")

class Child(Parent):
    def method(self):
        super().method()  # Call the parent's method
        print("Child method")

child = Child()
child.method()  # Output: Parent method\n              #         Child method"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Method Overriding"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Method overriding occurs when a derived class defines a method with the same name as a method in its base class. The derived class's method then overrides the base class's method, providing a specialized implementation."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"class Animal:
    def speak(self):
        print("Generic animal sound")

class Dog(Animal):
    def speak(self):
        print("Woof!")

class Cat(Animal):
    def speak(self):
        print("Meow!")

animal = Animal()
dog = Dog()
cat = Cat()

animal.speak()  # Output: Generic animal sound
dog.speak()     # Output: Woof!
cat.speak()     # Output: Meow!"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Multiple Inheritance"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Python supports multiple inheritance, where a class can inherit from multiple base classes. The order in which the base classes are specified in the class definition determines the method resolution order (MRO)."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"class Base1:
    def method1(self):
        print("Base1 method")

class Base2:
    def method2(self):
        print("Base2 method")

class Derived(Base1, Base2):
    pass

derived = Derived()
derived.method1()  # Output: Base1 method
derived.method2()  # Output: Base2 method"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Method Resolution Order (MRO)"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"The MRO determines the order in which Python searches for a method in a class hierarchy. You can view the MRO of a class using the `__mro__` attribute or the `mro()` method."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"class A:
    pass

class B(A):
    pass

class C(A):
    pass

class D(B, C):
    pass

print(D.__mro__) # Output: (<class '__main__.D'>, <class '__main__.B'>, <class '__main__.C'>, <class '__main__.A'>, <class 'object'>)"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Inheritance"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Code Reusability: Avoid redundant code by inheriting attributes and methods from existing classes."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Code Organization: Establish a clear hierarchy of classes, making your code more organized and maintainable."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Polymorphism: Enable polymorphism, where objects of different classes can be treated as objects of a common type."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inheritance is a powerful tool in Python for building complex and well-structured object-oriented programs. By understanding the concepts of base classes, derived classes, method overriding, and multiple inheritance, you can effectively leverage inheritance to create reusable, maintainable, and extensible code."}]
